description:
pkix-chain/src/lib.rs:367-383 (verify_chain's "Revocation coverage" section) and lines 527-536 (Verifier::verify_one's "Revocation coverage" section) contain near-identical text describing the same behaviour:

verify_chain (lines 367-383):
    /// # Revocation coverage
    ///
    /// Every certificate in `chain` is revocation-checked:
    ///
    /// - `chain[i]` where `chain[i+1]` exists: checked via
    ///   [`RevocationChecker::check_revocation`] with `chain[i+1]` as the issuer.
    /// - The last cert in `chain` (issued directly by the trust anchor): checked via
    ///   [`RevocationChecker::check_revocation_against_anchor`].
    ///
    /// The **default implementation** ...

verify_one (lines 527-536):
    /// # Revocation coverage
    ///
    /// Identical to [`verify_chain`]:
    ///
    /// - `chain[i]` where `chain[i + 1]` exists: checked via
    ///   [`RevocationChecker::check_revocation`] with `chain[i + 1]` as
    ///   the issuer.
    /// - The last cert in the validated chain (issued directly by the
    ///   trust anchor): checked via
    ///   [`RevocationChecker::check_revocation_against_anchor`].

verify_one points back at verify_chain ("Identical to [`verify_chain`]") AND restates the contract anyway. The two versions differ in minor ways:

- verify_chain says "in `chain`" (the input argument); verify_one says "in the validated chain" (post-AIA-augmentation).
- verify_chain's version includes a paragraph about default implementations and CrlChecker/OcspChecker overrides; verify_one's does not.
- Whitespace: `chain[i+1]` vs `chain[i + 1]`.

WHY IT MATTERS:

The slash-command checklist for docs as contract calls out:

> Doc comments that describe what the function does at the implementation level rather than what callers should rely on.

Plus the duplication itself is a maintenance hazard: a future refactor that changes revocation handling on the slow path (PKIX-sqek.1's AIA-augmented chain reassembly) updates one docstring and not the other; the contract diverges silently. Callers reading verify_chain see one rule, callers reading verify_one see another.

OPTIONS:

(a) **Single source of truth.** Have verify_one's rustdoc say only "Revocation coverage is identical to [`verify_chain`]; see that function for details." Delete the bullet list. One place to update if the contract changes.

(b) **Make verify_chain a thin wrapper that points at verify_one** (which is the actual implementation per line 397). The crate-level rustdoc at lines 65-66 already says "The free function [`verify_chain`] is a thin wrapper around [`Verifier::verify_one`]; both are zero-cost over the other." Have verify_chain's rustdoc point at verify_one for the substantive contract; remove the duplication on the wrapper.

(c) **Status quo.** Accept the duplication. Acceptable if you want each function's rustdoc to be self-contained for inline browsing.

Filed as P4 with opinion label per the slash-command instructions: this is a readability/maintenance preference, not an engineering risk that surfaces today. The "future divergence" scenario is real but speculative. Bikeshedding territory.
